---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import InstallBar from '../components/InstallBar.astro';
import Pipeline from '../components/Pipeline.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import McpButton from '../components/McpButton.astro';
import { SITE } from '../content/nav';
import { withBase } from '../lib/paths';
---

<DocsLayout
	title="KUYFI"
	eyebrow={`${SITE.version} · OPEN SOURCE`}
	description="Black-box security for Soroban."
>
	<p>
		Kuyfi analyzes deployed Soroban contracts from their on-chain interface, so you
		can inspect their attack surface, exercise inputs and review execution traces
		without requiring the source code.
	</p>

	<InstallBar />

	<div class="cta-row">
		<a class="btn btn--ghost" href={SITE.github} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"
			>VIEW ON GITHUB</a
		>
		<a class="btn btn--ghost" href={withBase('/quickstart')}>QUICKSTART</a>
		<McpButton variant="hero" href={withBase('/mcp')} />
	</div>

	<Callout type="tip" title="What Kuyfi is">
		<p>
			An automated <strong>black-box</strong> security terminal for Soroban. It is the
			layer <em>before</em> a formal audit — catch obvious surface issues on Testnet so
			auditors start from a cleaner baseline. It is <strong>not</strong> a certification
			and does not replace Ottersec, Veridise, CoinFabrik, or peers.
		</p>
	</Callout>

	<Pipeline />

	<h2>Why it exists</h2>
	<p>
		Formal audits need source, scheduling, and budget. Anyone can invoke a deployed
		Soroban contract; not everyone has its Rust source. Kuyfi starts from what is
		actually observable on-chain: WASM bytecode, the <code>contractspecv0</code> section,
		and runtime behavior under simulation and Testnet calls.
	</p>

	<pre><code>run Kuyfi → fix obvious issues → submit to a formal audit with a cleaner baseline</code></pre>

	<h2>What you get</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>OSINT Scanner</strong> — reconstruct functions, params, and UDTs from a
			Contract ID alone.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Chaos Monkey</strong> — type-aware fuzzing (math, auth, call-order, fee,
			liquidity), simulate-first, broadcast only when preflight succeeds.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>SecurityReport</strong> — one canonical object, exportable as JSON and PDF
			with execution evidence (hashes, ledgers, explorer URLs).
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>TUI + headless CLI</strong> — same engine for interactive and CI use.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Paid scan API</strong> — optional x402/Local402 endpoint for read-only
			scans (Chaos Monkey stays local).
		</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Hard constraints</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Stellar <strong>Testnet only</strong> — RPC is hardcoded to {SITE.rpc}.</li>
		<li>No private keys persisted — ephemeral Friendbot-funded keypairs in memory.</li>
		<li>Findings are <strong>signals</strong>, not proven exploits.</li>
		<li>Only test contracts you own or are authorized to test.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Docs map</h2>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th>Section</th>
				<th>Start here if you want…</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td><a href={withBase('/installation')}>Installation</a></td>
				<td>npm global install, Windows notes, from-source build</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><a href={withBase('/pipeline')}>Pipeline</a></td>
				<td>The Contract ID → report mental model</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><a href={withBase('/chaos-monkey')}>Chaos Monkey</a></td>
				<td>Vector families and simulate → broadcast lifecycle</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><a href={withBase('/for-llms')}>For LLMs</a></td>
				<td>Machine-readable copy, prompts, llms.txt</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td><a href={withBase('/mcp')}>MCP</a></td>
				<td>Future Model Context Protocol hook + Connect button</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
</DocsLayout>

<style>
	.cta-row {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		align-items: center;
		margin: 0 0 2rem;
	}

	.btn {
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		padding: 0.7rem 1.1rem;
		font-family: var(--font-display);
		font-size: 0.78rem;
		font-weight: 700;
		letter-spacing: 0.08em;
		text-decoration: none;
		border-radius: var(--radius);
	}

	.btn--ghost {
		border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.55);
		color: var(--text);
		background: transparent;
	}

	.btn--ghost:hover {
		border-color: var(--yellow);
		color: var(--yellow);
		text-decoration: none;
	}

	strong {
		color: var(--text);
	}
</style>
